AB  - Object detection in autonomous driving is a long-tail problem and there are always new corner cases to learn. To alleviate this problem, researchers have tried to leverage extended reality (XR) technologies to simulate different scenarios. Meanwhile, generative AI has made remarkable progress and can generate high-quality images based on text prompts. In this paper, we explore the use of both generative and XR data for training detectors to recognize real traffic objects. We propose Adaptive Data Valuation Reinforcement Learning (ADVRL), which progressively learns a data valuation network to evaluate synthetic training images. ADVRL dynamically adjusts the weights of misclassified validation data, and re-learns to select most valuable training samples from XR and generative datasets. Experiments demonstrated that ADVRL can learn to recognize new classes while increasing object detection accuracy by up to 11%. Qualitative analysis showed that our detector can identify objects missed by commercial software such as Tesla Vision.
ER  - 

