AB  - The evolution of Extended Reality (XR) headsets into all-day wearable platforms presents a profound opportunity for affective lifelogging. However, this is hindered by the ‘Dynamic Baseline Problem,’ which complicates emotional state detection. To address this, we propose the DACC (Dynamic Affective Capture & Comprehension) framework, a novel hybrid and mixed-initiative architecture. Its real-time EEG trigger addresses the dynamic baseline by detecting significant deviations from a user’s constantly updating contextual neutral state, enabling immediate, automatic capture. Complementing this with user-initiated capture to ensure agency, DACC employs a powerful multimodal foundation model for the post-hoc semantic analysis of captured moments. This separation of detection from comprehension outlines a new HCI paradigm: ‘emotional reminiscence,’ allowing users to review their lives based on feeling, not just chronology.
ER  - 

