AB  - The aging global population has led to a demand for intelligent assistive technologies that promote safe and independent mobility. This work introduces a modular software architecture that integrates Large Language Models (LLMs) and Augmented Reality (AR) into a robotic walker to provide context-aware navigation for older adults. By leveraging the Model Context Protocol (MCP), the system bridges high-level semantic reasoning with low-level robotic control. A priority-based Behavior Tree ensures operational safety by managing sensor feedback independently of the non-deterministic latency associated with cloud-based LLM inference. Experimental trials with six volunteers resulted in successful navigation for all participants, with an average total response time of 2.94 seconds. The results validate the system's architecture, showing that a 2 Hz local safety loop effectively complements the three-second cognitive processing delay. While qualitative feedback confirmed the interface's intuitiveness and a strong user preference for auditory guidance, the findings also highlighted challenges such as auditory fatigue and AR field-of-view constraints. This research contributes to safer, more natural human-robot interaction in assistive mobility devices.
