AB  - Extended Reality (XR) is increasingly positioned as a transformative technological paradigm across diverse sectors, including education, healthcare, entertainment, and industry. Central to delivering immersive and context-aware XR experiences is the integration of Computer Vision (CV), which enables systems to perceive, interpret, and interact with realworld environments in real-time. As the adoption of XR accelerates on mobile platforms, Android has emerged as a critical enabler, due to its global reach, open ecosystem, and rich set of developer tools. This paper presents a comprehensive review of the technical foundations and current research surrounding CV-enabled XR applications on Android. Emphasis is placed on key components such as ARCore, OpenCV, and TensorFlow Lite, as well as foundational techniques including SLAM, depth estimation, and real-time object detection. Relevant comparisons with platforms such as ARKit and HoloLens are briefly discussed, with a focus on highlighting Android-specific characteristics. The paper also discusses technical challenges, such as latency, device heterogeneity, and on-device computational constraints, and outlines future directions involving generative AI, user behavior analysis, and intelligent XR systems. Ultimately, the convergence of CV and XR on the Android platform is shown through an application, signifying a powerful combination for enabling scalable, adaptive, and accessible immersive experiences.
ER  - 

