AB  - The low-risk and cost-effective nature of simulated environments makes them a great alternative to real-world testing. However, even the best simulators fail to grasp the full complexity of real environments. Moreover, there is a resurgence of a need for user-focused systems, which allow them to interact with technology in a simpler manner. We propose a high-fidelity simulation framework that combines realistic physics, interactive scene adaptation, and immersive visualization in robotics applications. Our approach adopts the concept of a digital model, omitting real-time data exchange. The framework integrates NVIDIA's Isaac Sim for high-fidelity simulation, a custom vision system Optimus for real-time object recognition, and an LLM to enable scene manipulation through natural language commands. Users can dynamically modify the scene using text-based instructions, while the environment can be visualized through a VR headset, offering a more immersive interface. A demo in an industrial waste management scenario was implemented and described to validate the idea. Although the system as a whole performed successfully, we identified several limitations, including challenges in VR integration, prompt sensitivity, and lack of formal validation.
ER  - 

