AB  - This paper presents an opportunistic, frame-first augmented reality (AR) pipeline for first-responder support that departs from scan-first approaches. The system anchors real-time object detections into world coordinates via the Meta Quest 3 depth API and maintains an egocentric scene graph encoding human–object and object–object relations. A multimodal vision–language model (VLM) operates over this structured snapshot to infer geometric relations, functional groupings, and semantic context beyond object detection alone. Unlike scan-first designs, our pipeline avoids long initialization delays by incrementally building spatial awareness from partial observations while leveraging schematic priors. We report stage-wise and end-to-end latency, and comparative evaluations showing that geometry-augmented VLM reasoning improves spatial accuracy and relational reliability relative to VLM-only baselines. Together, these findings demonstrate that grounding semantics in geometry offers a practical path to responsive, low-latency AR assistance in safety-critical scenarios.
ER  - 

